# How line endings are decided, so that it is not decided per machine.
#
# Without this file the answer comes from each developer's core.autocrlf. Here
# it was true, which checks files out with CRLF while the repository stores LF —
# and every tool that writes a file with LF then leaves it half one and half the
# other. Anchors stop matching, diffs fill with lines nobody touched, and a
# scripted edit fails for a reason that has nothing to do with what it changed.
#
# The repository already stores LF for all 205 text files, so this settles the
# working copy rather than rewriting history: text is checked out as LF too.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the two kinds Windows itself reads. A batch file with LF endings can
# parse differently, and there is nothing to gain by finding out where.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never looks for line endings in them. The PNGs already contain
# byte pairs that look like CRLF, which is exactly the mistake to prevent.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jar binary
*.exe binary
*.pfx binary
*.kiwi binary
